CLI Stdout and Stderr
===============================================================================

The CLI host exposes stdout and stderr as send-style output contexts.

```mech
+> @out := cli/stdout
+> @err := cli/stderr

@out/line <- "Hello"
@err/text <- "warning"
@out/bytes <- [0u8 128u8 255u8]
```

1. Paths
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Supported paths:

| Path | Behavior |
|------|----------|
| `text` | Write text without adding a newline. |
| `line` | Write text with a trailing newline. |
| `bytes` | Write a canonical U8 row of shape 1,N unchanged, without a suffix. |

2. Operations
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`cli/stdout` and `cli/stderr` support output writes. Use send syntax with `<-` for stream output.

The text and line paths require scalar Strings. The bytes path accepts exactly
a rank-two U8 matrix with one row, including an empty 1,0 row. Other element
kinds, nested arrays, columns and multiple rows are rejected. A schema with a
dynamic row extent is checked against the real payload before preparation.

Byte paths require explicit write grants naming bytes. A text grant supplies no
authority over bytes. The existing instance aliases and read-only/send-only
directions apply to every path.

Output is validated and retained as an after-commit effect. Preparation or a
failed source turn emits no output; successful delivery preserves source order
and counts every byte in the existing effect-cost contract. The standard backend
writes all bytes and flushes, reporting write or flush errors. As with text, a
delivery failure after commit cannot undo bytes already written to an external
stream. At-most-once delivery and shutdown do not replay failed terminal output.

Custom backends implement write_stdout_bytes() and write_stderr_bytes() for raw
delivery. The default methods report unavailable; they never decode bytes or
fall back to text. Existing text-only backends retain their text methods.
